    "title": "Status of U.S., Iran ceasefire in limbo as Strait of Hormuz remains unopened | CBC",
    "text": "Fragile truce holds as key disputes remain unresolvedThe two-week conditional ceasefire between the United States and Iran got off to an unsteady start Wednesday as the two sides disagreed on two points undermining the agreement: access to the Strait of Hormuz and Israel's punishing attacks on Lebanon.Early Wednesday, Israel's heaviest strikes of the war on Lebanon reportedly killed many dozens of people — including civilians and children — in densely populated areas of the country. The United Nations' human rights chief said \"such carnage, within hours of agreeing to a ceasefire with Iran, defies belief.\"The attacks drew retaliatory threats from Iran, which said the destruction violated the ceasefire plan with the U.S. announced just hours before. The Trump administration insisted Lebanon was never part of the agreement — though Pakistan, which negotiated the ceasefire, said Lebanon was included.The timeline for reopening the Strait of Hormuz is also in dispute. The critical shipping lane remained shut to vessels without a permit on Wednesday. Major shipping companies kept their vessels in place, as Iran had previously warned any ship entering without permission could be \"targeted and destroyed.\"U.S. Vice-President JD Vance will lead peace talks in Pakistan on Saturday, according to the White House. Iran's delegation will include Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi and parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf.Shortly before leaving Budapest to return to Washington tonight, Vance declined to say whether Trump's threat that \"a whole civilization will die\" was off the table now that a ceasefire, however fragile, was in place. He warned the president could restart the war and face \"serious consequences\" if he believed Iran wasn't holding up its end of the deal.\"If they don't give us what we need, then I think it's going to be bad, but I'm optimistic the Iranians are going to be smart and that they're going to negotiate in good faith,\" Vance said.Late in the evening, Trump repeated his criticism of NATO on social media after meeting with alliance Secretary General Mark Rutte and alluded to his previous threats to annex Greenland. He did not comment on confusion surrounding the terms of the ceasefire with Iran.",
